  "problem": "Evaluate the Hessian determinant of $f$ at $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $H(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)\\,=$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,H(0,0)\\,=$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "We computed the second partial derivatives in part 5. So, the second derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}24{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}-16 & 32xy \\\\ 32xy & 16{x}^{2}+12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)=[\\begin{aligned}32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 44\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)=32\\cdot 44-0\\cdot 0=1\\,408.\n$$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=-16\\cdot 12-0\\cdot 0=-192.\n$$",
